.Dd April 19, 2026
.Dt PROWL-UNIT 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm prowl-unit
.Nd prowld job configuration format
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A unit file is a declarative configuration file that defines a job managed by
.Xr prowld 8 .
Jobs can be long-running daemons, oneshot tasks, schedule-driven timers,
or on-demand services triggered by socket activity.
.Pp
Unit files are parsed by
.Xr libucl 3
and support UCL, JSON, and simplified YAML-like syntax.
Files must have the
.Pa .unit
extension and be located in one of the search directories described in
.Xr prowld 8 .
.Sh SCHEMA
The following fields are recognised at the top level of a unit file:
.Ss Identity
.Bl -tag -width "description"
.It Va label
.Pq required, string
Unique reverse-DNS identifier for the job,
e.g.,
.Dq org.midnightbsd.sshd .
Used for dependency resolution and management via
.Xr prowlctl 8 .
.It Va description
.Pq string
Human-readable description of the service.
.It Va type
.Pq string
One of:
.Bl -tag -width "oneshot" -compact
.It Cm daemon
A long-running process (default).
.It Cm oneshot
A task that runs once and is considered complete on successful exit.
.It Cm timer
A job activated by a schedule or interval.
.It Cm socket
A job activated by incoming socket connections.
.El
.It Va rc_name
.Pq string
The prefix used for the
.Va _enable
variable in
.Pa /etc/rc.conf .
Defaults to the last component of
.Va label .
.El
.Ss Execution
.Bl -tag -width "working_directory"
.It Va program
.Pq string
Absolute path to the executable.
Required unless
.Va arguments[0]
is an absolute path.
.It Va arguments
.Pq array of strings
Argument vector passed to the process.
The executable name is automatically prepended as
.Vt argv[0] .
.It Va stop_arguments
.Pq array of strings
Argument vector for a command that stops the job, run with the same
.Va program ,
user and environment.
It is used when the job is stopped while it has no process of its own, as
for a
.Cm oneshot
that has completed: the job is then
.Em stopping
until the command exits and
.Em stopped
afterwards, whatever its exit status.
Without it, stopping a completed oneshot does nothing.
.It Va reload_arguments , check_arguments , resync_arguments
.Pq arrays of strings
Argument vectors for the commands behind
.Dq prowlctl reload ,
.Dq prowlctl check
and
.Dq prowlctl resync .
Each is run with the same
.Va program ,
user and environment as the job, and
.Xr prowlctl 8
prints what it writes to standard output and standard error and exits with
its status.
A command that runs longer than 30 seconds is killed, and the first 8192
bytes of its output are kept.
Without
.Va reload_arguments
a running job gets SIGHUP.
.Ic reload
and
.Ic resync
need a job that has been started; a
.Ic check
may run at any time.
.It Va environment
.Pq object
Key/value pairs set in the child's environment.
The process does not inherit the environment of
.Xr prowld 8 .
.It Va working_directory
.Pq string
Directory to
.Xr chdir 2
into before execution.
.It Va root_directory
.Pq string
Directory to
.Xr chroot 2
into.
Requires
.Va user
to be set for security.
.It Va user , Va group
.Pq string
User and group names to drop privileges to.
.It Va umask
.Pq string
Octal umask (e.g.,
.Dq 0022 ) .
.El
.Ss Lifecycle
.Bl -tag -width "throttle_interval"
.It Va run_at_load
.Pq boolean
If true, start the job automatically when loaded.
.It Va keep_alive
.Pq boolean or object
Restart policy.
See
.Xr prowld 8
for detailed object keys:
.Va always ,
.Va crashed ,
.Va successful_exit .
.It Va throttle_interval
.Pq integer
Minimum seconds between restart attempts.
Default: 10.
.It Va exit_timeout
.Pq integer
Seconds to wait after
.Dv SIGTERM
before sending
.Dv SIGKILL .
Default: 20.
.It Va shutdown_wait
.Pq boolean
Wait for clean exit during system shutdown.
.It Va required_files
.Pq array of strings
Absolute paths of files that must exist and be readable for the job to
start, such as its configuration file.
If one is missing the job is not started: it enters the
.Dq failed
state, the reason is logged, and
.Cm prowlctl start
reports an error.
Starting it again after creating the file works.
At most 8 paths are allowed.
A relative path, or too many, makes
.Xr prowld 8
refuse the unit rather than weaken the check.
.It Va required_modules
.Pq array of strings
Kernel modules to load before the job starts, as
.Va required_modules
does in
.Xr rc.subr 8 .
An entry is the file to load, or
.Dq file:module
when the module has a different name.
A module that is already loaded or built in is left alone; if one cannot be
loaded the job does not run.
At most 8 entries are allowed.
.It Va instances_var
Makes the file a template; see
.Sx TEMPLATES .
The name of an
.Xr rc.conf 5
variable holding a space-separated list of instance names.
.It Va default_instance
For a template, the instance created when
.Va instances_var
is empty or unset.
.It Va nojail
.Pq boolean
If true, the job is never started when
.Xr prowld 8
runs inside a jail.
Use it for services that need the real hardware or kernel state,
like
.Xr devd 8 .
.El
.Ss Dependencies
.Bl -tag -width "requires"
.It Va requires
.Pq array of strings
Capability names that must be running for this job to start.
.It Va wants
.Pq array of strings
Like
.Va requires ,
but a provider that is disabled, masked or failed does not hold the job
back, and a capability that nothing provides is ignored.
This matches the way
.Xr rc 8
treats REQUIRE, so units converted from rc.d scripts use it.
.It Va provides
.Pq array of strings
Capabilities this job satisfies (e.g.,
.Dq network ) .
.It Va before , Va after
.Pq array of strings
Explicit ordering constraints against other job labels.
.El
.Ss Scheduling (Timers)
The
.Va schedule
field is an object used by
.Cm type :
.Cm timer
jobs:
.Bl -tag -width "on_boot_delay"
.It Va interval
.Pq integer
Recurring interval in seconds.
.It Va on_boot_delay
.Pq integer
One-shot delay after the daemon starts.
.It Va calendar
.Pq object
Cron-like triggers.
Fields:
.Va minute
(0-59),
.Va hour
(0-23),
.Va day
(1-31),
.Va month
(1-12),
.Va weekday
(0-6, Sunday is 0).
Fields support integers or the
.Dq *
wildcard.
.El
.Ss Socket Activation
The
.Va sockets
field is an object mapping logical names to socket descriptions.
Keys in each description:
.Va type
(stream, dgram, seqpacket),
.Va family
(inet, inet6, unix),
.Va path
(AF_UNIX only),
.Va port
(inet only),
.Va host
(bind address),
.Va backlog .
.Sh RC.CONF VARIABLES
.Va program ,
.Va arguments ,
.Va stop_arguments ,
.Va reload_arguments ,
.Va check_arguments ,
.Va resync_arguments
and
.Va required_files
may refer to variables from
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
so that a unit does not need a shell script to read them.
The variables are those of the unit's
.Va rc_name :
.Pa /etc/defaults/rc.conf ,
.Pa /etc/rc.conf ,
.Pa /etc/rc.conf.local ,
and
.Pa /etc/rc.conf.d/ Ns Ar rc_name
and
.Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.conf.d/ Ns Ar rc_name ,
with later files overriding earlier ones.
They are read each time the job is started.
.Bl -tag -width "@name?text@" -compact
.It Li @name@
The value, empty if the variable is not set.
.It Li @name:-default@
The value, or default if it is empty.
.It Li @name?text@
text if the value is
.Dv YES ,
.Dv TRUE ,
.Dv ON
or
.Dv 1 ,
and nothing otherwise.
.It Li @@
A literal
.Ql @ .
.El
.Pp
An argument that is only one reference becomes as many arguments as the
value has words, none if it is empty, the way an unquoted
.Ev $flags
does in
.Xr sh 1 .
Anywhere else the value is put in place as one word.
Arguments that contain a newline are shell scripts and are left alone.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
program = "/usr/sbin/ntpd";
arguments = ["-d", "@ntpd_flags@"];
.Ed
.Sh TEMPLATES
A unit file whose name ends in
.Pa @.unit
is a template.
It is not loaded as a job itself.
Every
.Dq %i
in the file is replaced by an instance name and
.Dq %%
by a single
.Dq % ,
before the file is parsed, so the label, the program, the arguments and
the other fields can all depend on the instance.
A template's label must contain
.Dq %i
and the name of an instance may only use letters, digits and
.Dq _ ,
.Dq -
and
.Dq \&. .
.Pp
At start-up prowld creates one instance for each name in the
.Xr rc.conf 5
variable given by
.Va instances_var ,
or the
.Va default_instance
if that variable is empty.
Any other instance is created when it is first named, as in
.Dq prowlctl start pflog@pf3 .
An instance can be addressed by its label, such as
.Dq org.midnightbsd.pflog@pf1 ,
or by the shorter
.Dq pflog@pf1 .
Drop-in files for a template are read from
.Pa <name>@.d .
.Sh EXAMPLES
A simple periodic cleanup task:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "label": "org.midnightbsd.cleanup",
  "type": "timer",
  "program": "/usr/libexec/mycleanup",
  "schedule": {
    "interval": 3600
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A daemon with dependencies and socket activation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "label": "org.midnightbsd.myservice",
  "description": "My Network Service",
  "program": "/usr/local/bin/mysrv",
  "requires": ["network"],
  "sockets": {
    "http": { "port": 80, "family": "inet" }
  }
}
.Ed
.Sh FILES
Units for base system services are installed in
.Pa /etc/prowld/units.d/ .
Each replaces the matching
.Pa /etc/rc.d
script when
.Xr prowld 8
manages the system, and each honours the service's
.Va _enable
and
.Va _flags
variables from
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
